      "text": "To find the common values of the arithmetic progressions \\(\\{2, 5, 8, 11, \\ldots\\}\\) and \\(\\{3, 10, 17, 24, \\ldots\\}\\), we need to first express the general terms of these sequences.\n\nThe first sequence has a first term \\(a_1 = 2\\) and a common difference \\(d_1 = 3\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of this sequence can be written as:\n\\[ a_n = 2 + (n-1) \\cdot 3 = 3n - 1. \\]\n\nThe second sequence has a first term \\(b_1 = 3\\) and a common difference \\(d_2 = 7\\). The \\(m\\)-th term of this sequence can be written as:\n\\[ b_m = 3 + (m-1) \\cdot 7 = 7m - 4. \\]\n\nWe need to find the values of \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) such that \\(a_n = b_m\\). Therefore, we set the two expressions equal to each other:\n\\[ 3n - 1 = 7m - 4. \\]\nRearranging the equation, we get:\n\\[ 3n - 7m = -3. \\]\nTo find the solutions to this equation, we use the method of finding particular solutions and then the general solution for the linear homogeneous Diophantine equation.\n\nThe particular solution to the equation \\(3n - 7m = -3\\) can be found by trying small values. Let's try \\(n = 1\\) and see if we get an integer value for \\(m\\):\n\\[ 3(1) - 7m = -3 \\implies 3 - 7m = -3 \\implies -7m = -6 \\implies m = \\frac{6}{7}, \\]\nwhich is not an integer. So, let's try another value, \\(n = 4\\):\n\\[ 3(4) - 7m = -3 \\implies 12 - 7m = -3 \\implies -7m = -15 \\implies m = \\frac{15}{7}, \\]\nwhich is also not an integer. Let's try \\(n = 7\\):\n\\[ 3(7) - 7m = -3 \\implies 21 - 7m = -3 \\implies -7m = -24 \\implies m = \\frac{24}{7}, \\]\nwhich is not an integer. Let's try \\(n = 10\\):\n\\[ 3(10) - 7m = -3 \\implies 30 - 7m = -3 \\implies -7m = -33 \\implies m = \\frac{33}{7}, \\]\nwhich is not an integer. Let's try \\(n = 13\\):\n\\[ 3(13) - 7m = -3 \\implies 39 - 7m = -3 \\implies -7m = -42 \\implies m = 6, \\]\nwhich is an integer. So, \\(n = 13\\) and \\(m = 6\\) is a particular solution. The general solution to the equation \\(3n - 7m = -3\\) is given by:\n\\[ n = 13 + 7k, \\]\n\\[ m = 6 + 3k, \\]\nfor any integer \\(k\\).\n\nWe need to find the largest value of \\(a_n\\) that is less than 500. The \\(n\\)-th term is \\(a_n = 3n - 1\\). Setting \\(a_n < 500\\), we get:\n\\[ 3n - 1 < 500 \\implies 3n < 501 \\implies n < 167. \\]\nThe largest integer \\(n\\) is 166. Substituting \\(n = 166\\) into the expression for \\(a_n\\), we get:\n\\[ a_{166} = 3(166) - 1 = 498 - 1 = 497. \\]\nTherefore, the largest value less than 500 that the two arithmetic progressions have in common is:\n\\[ \\boxed{497}. \\]",
