      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to identify the common terms of the two arithmetic progressions and then find the largest term less than 500.\n\nThe first arithmetic progression (AP1) is given by:\n\\[ a_1 = 2, \\quad d_1 = 3 \\]\n\nThe second arithmetic progression (AP2) is given by:\n\\[ a_2 = 3, \\quad d_2 = 7 \\]\n\nThe general term of an arithmetic progression can be expressed as:\n\\[ a_n = a + (n-1)d \\]\n\nFor AP1, the \\(n\\)-th term is:\n\\[ a_1^n = 2 + (n-1) \\cdot 3 = 3n - 1 \\]\n\nFor AP2, the \\(n\\)-th term is:\n\\[ a_2^n = 3 + (n-1) \\cdot 7 = 7n - 4 \\]\n\nWe need to find the common terms of these two sequences. Therefore, we set the general terms equal to each other:\n\\[ 3n - 1 = 7m - 4 \\]\n\\[ 3n - 7m = -3 \\]\n\nWe need to solve this Diophantine equation. First, we find a particular solution. By inspection, we see that \\( n = 4 \\) and \\( m = 1 \\) is a solution:\n\\[ 3(4) - 7(1) = 12 - 7 = 5 \\]\nHowever, we need \\(-3\\), so we multiply the particular solution by \\(-1\\):\n\\[ n = 4, \\quad m = -1 \\]\n\nThe general solution to the equation \\( 3n - 7m = -3 \\) is given by:\n\\[ n = 4 - 7k, \\quad m = -1 + 3k \\]\nfor any integer \\( k \\).\n\nSubstituting \\( n = 4 - 7k \\) into the general term of AP1:\n\\[ a_n = 3(4 - 7k) - 1 = 12 - 21k - 1 = 11 - 21k \\]\n\nWe need the largest value of \\( a_n \\) that is less than 500:\n\\[ 11 - 21k < 500 \\]\n\\[ -21k < 489 \\]\n\\[ k > -\\frac{489}{21} \\approx -23.2857 \\]\n\nThe largest integer \\( k \\) is \\(-23\\). Substituting \\( k = -23 \\) into the general term:\n\\[ a_n = 11 - 21(-23) = 11 + 483 = 494 \\]\n\nThus, the largest value less than 500 that is common to both arithmetic progressions is:\n\\[ \\boxed{494} \\]",
